od  State and we'll continue to discuss this  across government and indeed with  parliamentary colleagues  um over the few weeks ahead you say in a  good state but the director of The  Institute for fiscal studies is saying  this morning that there's a 60 billion  pound Black Hole uh in your figures and  that's either going to be done to sort  it out with a hatchet to public spending  or the equivalent of a Tempe increase in  the basic rate of tax which one well I  think the ifs obviously does its own  modeling the government works with the  bank of England and the obr on these  measures and that's uh What uh treasury  has been working on and doing different  discussions with different departments  but I think the ifs also pointed out if  we don't grow then this problem will get  worse and worse and that's why very  clearly the Prime Minister and  chancellor set out a plan for growth uh  a lot of that of course was the  important element of giving Assurance to  household and to businesses about what  support they would get on energy costs  much of which is not in the direct  control of people we've already seen an  impact in the reduction of inflation uh  forecasts just from the measures that  we've taken but to consider we've had  this Aftershock of covid we're still not  quite yet got the global supply chain  going again and we still have these huge  problems on energy security which is  affecting not just this hasn't been  affecting just this country but of  course also countries right across  Europe and elsewhere in the world but  people should be assured we have the  second lowest debt to GDP ratio in the G  UH 60 billion pound black hole though um  how are you going well I'm I'm just uh  flagging that ifs has come up with its  own modeling is it right and the chance  that's uh not for me to say that's their  forecasts uh the chancellor will be  speaking at the end of the month uh with  the medium-term fiscal plan okay but  suggestions are that you will have to  find on their figures for now we'll hear  yours as you say by the end of the month  if you don't  um fix these figures with an increase in  basic rate of income tax you'll need to  reduce the budget of every Department  except the NHS and defense by 15 is  there that much fat to get rid of well  I'm just not going to get into  hypotheticals k um the chancellor is  working on that uh with uh as the  aspects the obr other departments right  now to go through um but I the  government is united we have to get  growth otherwise uh and that will help  us to fund our public sectors in the  future as well okay and so you're not  going to get into figures we'll just  have to wait and see till the 31st of  October as I said the Market's getting  jittery again one wonders what they  might do between now and then what we do  know is that Santander are saying that  they're setting um just looking at the  exact quote from their boss saying  they're having to set aside more money  for defaults uh because they think that  people are just not going to be able to  afford their mortgages going forward how  do you reassure them otherwise well the  bank of England sets the interest rates  for the country and it's for their  projection to decide they we've had  historically low interest rates for many  years now and of course they're going  through covert there was a lot of work  done when many people either weren't uh  were benefiting from furlough or indeed  were not who had to be helped with that  I'm just flagging That Was Then That Was  Then historically what the government  did to help people Bank of England the  bank of England sets the interest rates  so it'll be what responsibility should  this government take well the backing of  England sets interest rates um I'm not  aware that any other commentator once  the government to start setting interest  rates again  um and I doubt uh Santander does as well  but the sorts of policies that we've  shown like our support to both  households and businesses in substantial  energy package building on the 37  billion pounds that we had already  talking about buildings you're talking  about ditching home targets for  affordable homes why are you doing that  I'm not aware of any of that that isn't  true I'm not that isn't true that  apparently ministers have drawn up plans  to exempt developers from having to  build affordable homes to scrap  Environmental Protections and to allow  people to add extensions without  permission that according to Simon Clark  that's not right I'm not aware of that  uh policy but uh if you want to ask  Simon Clark about policies I guess the  Deputy Prime Minister with all due  respect  um well you're just throwing comments at  me okay  um I'm not aware of there's not  something that's been discussed around  I'm not aware of uh specific things like  that no okay how interesting Amazon  Secretary of State for health and that's  uh I have come on today to talk about  what we're doing to help tackle the  backlogs and I'm pleased to be  announcing a further 10 Community  diagnostic Centers to be opened I'm  actually opening one formally today in  London but places in Burnley uh places  in uh merseyside uh places in the Isle  of Wight will be getting a diagnostic  center because we know getting on and  delivering for patients is really  important and those Diagnostics are a  key part of making sure whether we know  if people need treatment or not and that  is one of the critical backlogs that we  need to get fixed  um also talking about National Insurance  rise being scrapped today in Parliament  that's at 36 billion pounds over three  years that will be gone how will you  protect the NHS given that that money  was going to be spent on the NHS and  social care well that was going to be in  effect a hypothecated levy that was set  aside the Prime Minister said clearly  during her leadership campaign she  didn't think it was right to put up  taxes and so so this will be funded mo